# gen-sql.aon --- CREATE TABLE statements from model.aon.
#
# Reads the `sql` slice and ignores `go` entirely. Identifiers are
# QUOTED, because `order` is a reserved word in SQL and a generator
# that emits it bare produces a file that does not parse -- the
# reserved-word problem every code generator meets, answered here by
# always quoting rather than by carrying a word list.
#
# THE TRAILING COMMA IS THE POINT. Every column line ends in `,`,
# including the last, so this output is NOT valid SQL. Nothing in the
# language can fix it: a separator BETWEEN N items is a fold, and
# Aontu has no fold over strings -- `sum` is numeric, `+` does not
# reduce a list, and indexed concatenation needs the arity known in
# advance. `join(bag, sep)` is G9 phase 2 and it is the whole
# difference. See README.md, "The one missing primitive".
#
# `rows` IS NOT HIDDEN, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The obvious spelling
# wraps the staged intermediate in `hide()` so the scaffolding stays
# out of the generated value. It does not survive the parity probe:
# inside `hide()` the Go port never resolves the spread template's
# `.n`, so the transform generates in TypeScript and refuses in Go
# with `mapval_no_gen` -- opposite exit codes on the same document
# (use-cases/BUGS.md 63). Left unhidden, both ports agree byte for
# byte, which check.sh asserts. `rows` therefore rides in the
# evaluated value and the fold ignores it.
@"./model.aon"

units: [&: {
  head: `CREATE TABLE "` + .sql + `" (`
  rows: [&: {
    out: `  "` + .sql + `" ` +
         match(.t, "string", `TEXT`, "integer", `BIGINT`) +
         match(.req, true, ` NOT NULL`, false, ``) + `,`
  }] & .fields
  body: pick(.rows, out)
  tail: `);`
}] & $.records
